{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"pilot","tool":"container_exec","arguments":{"command":"cat '/work/ProbabilityTheoryFormalization/SOURCE.tex'","timeout_seconds":30},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.1879999999946449, \"stdout\": \"% BEGIN EXACT CATALOG SOURCE: prob_13_9\\n\\\\textbf{13.9.} Suppose (X n)n\\\\geq0 is a sequence of random variables defined on a probability\\n\\nspace (we take X 0 = 0 as the initial rv.)For n \\\\geq 1, let\\n\\nYn \\\\coloneqqX n -E [Xn\\\\vertX0,X 1,...,X n- 1].\\n\\nThe random variable Y n can be interpreted as the new information contained in X n\\n\\nrelative to the information contained in the past X 0,...,X n- 1Let Sn denote the\\n\\npartial sum S n \\\\coloneqqY 1 +Y 2 +\\\\cdot\\\\cdot\\\\cdot+ Yn for n \\\\geq 1 and S 0 = 0. Show that (S n)n\\\\geq0 is a\\n\\nmartingale relative to the filtration (\\\\sigma(X 1,X 2,...,X n))n\\\\geq0.\\n% END EXACT CATALOG SOURCE: prob_13_9\\n\", \"stderr\": \"\", \"stdout_truncated\": false, \"stderr_truncated\": false, \"raw_stdout\": {\"bytes\": 629, \"sha256\": \"a560db2dd7a38b9b22e7c4cfdb191d6f98c180e5d6cc8561ff2cd773e2fb9c1b\"}, \"raw_stderr\": {\"bytes\": 0, \"sha256\": \"e3b0c44298fc1c149afbf4c8996fb92427ae41e4649b934ca495991b7852b855\"}}"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
